<p>Ask five automation agencies how much their work costs, and you&#39;ll get five vague answers: &quot;It depends,&quot; &quot;Every project is different,&quot; and my personal favorite, &quot;Let&#39;s get on a call and find out.&quot;</p>
<p>Let me do something the industry usually won&#39;t: give you the actual numbers.</p>
<p>I&#39;m Alec Kennedy, founder of The One Group, an AI automation agency for small and mid-size businesses. We publish our pricing because businesses deserve to budget before they talk to us. This post breaks down what AI automation really costs in 2026 — audits, implementation, coaching, and monthly retainers — and, more importantly, how to figure out what <em>you</em> should be willing to pay based on your own ROI.</p>
<h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>A small business should expect to spend anywhere from <strong>a few hundred dollars a month</strong> for a single, simple automation up to <strong>$10,000+</strong> for a full build-out of their operations. Most service-based businesses land in a sensible middle band once you count the initial work plus ongoing maintenance.</p>
<p>But a dollar figure without context is useless. So let&#39;s break it down by what you&#39;re actually buying.</p>
<h2>The Four Price Bands That Actually Matter</h2>
<h3>1. The AI Visibility Audit: $2,500</h3>
<p>Before anyone should build anything, they need to know what to build. An audit is a deep look at your business — your workflows, your tools, your bottlenecks — that produces a prioritized roadmap of what to automate first, second, and third.</p>
<p><strong>What you get:</strong> a documented map of your highest-ROI automation opportunities, with the expected time savings and revenue impact of each, ranked so you can decide what&#39;s worth doing and what isn&#39;t.</p>
<p><strong>Who it&#39;s for:</strong> any business that knows it&#39;s wasting time on busywork but isn&#39;t sure where to start. An audit is the cheapest insurance against throwing money at the wrong tool.</p>
<h3>2. Implementation: $2,500–$10,000</h3>
<p>This is the actual build. An implementation project takes the systems identified in your audit (or that you already know you need) and makes them real — the AI agent that answers your calls, the follow-up sequence that runs on autopilot, the bookkeeping automation that reconciles itself.</p>
<p><strong>Why the range is so wide:</strong> it depends on complexity and scope.</p>
<ul>
<li>A <strong>single, well-defined automation</strong> (say, an AI scheduling and reminder system) lands at the low end — around <strong>$2,500–$4,000</strong>.</li>
<li>A <strong>moderate build</strong> (lead capture + follow-up + scheduling across a few tools) runs <strong>$4,000–$7,000</strong>.</li>
<li>A <strong>full operations overhaul</strong> — multiple connected systems that talk to each other across your whole business — reaches <strong>$7,000–$10,000</strong>.</li>
</ul>
<p>The honest truth: if an agency quotes you &quot;implementation&quot; without being able to tell you roughly which end of this band you&#39;re on, keep shopping.</p>
<h3>3. AI Coaching: $1,500–$7,500</h3>
<p>Not every business needs a full custom build. Plenty of owners want to learn to run these tools themselves. Coaching gets your team — usually you, your ops person, or your assistant — fluent in the AI systems you&#39;re using so you don&#39;t stay dependent on an agency forever.</p>
<p><strong>What you get:</strong> hands-on training, templates, and a system you actually understand. Programs range from a short focused session (<strong>$1,500–$3,000</strong>) up to an in-depth engagement for a team that wants to build its own capability (<strong>$4,000–$7,500</strong>).</p>
<p>Coaching is a great middle option for businesses that want autonomy. It&#39;s also the piece most buyers forget to budget for — don&#39;t.</p>
<h3>4. The Monthly Retainer: from $297/month</h3>
<p>Automations aren&#39;t a &quot;set it and forget it&quot; thing forever. Tools update, business processes change, and someone needs to keep the systems healthy and improving. That&#39;s the ongoing piece.</p>
<p><strong>Competitor Intel, our simplest ongoing product, is $297/month</strong> — automated monitoring of your competitors&#39; pricing, positioning, and offers, delivered in a weekly digest. It&#39;s a perfect example of a low-cost, high-value automation that runs forever.</p>
<p>Full retainer relationships for ongoing management, optimization, and new automations scale up from there based on how many systems you&#39;re running and how much support you need. The point is: plan for a recurring cost, not just a one-time build.</p>
<h2>A Simple ROI Calculator: Should You Automate?</h2>
<p>Here&#39;s the math that should drive every decision — and it&#39;s embarrassingly simple.</p>
<p><strong>X hours saved per week × hourly cost of that person × 52 weeks = annual savings</strong></p>
<p>Let me walk through it with real numbers.</p>
<p>Say your office manager spends <strong>6 hours a week</strong> on appointment reminders, invoice chasing, and manual follow-ups. Their loaded cost is <strong>$30/hour</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>6 hours × $30 = <strong>$180/week</strong> of busywork</li>
<li>$180 × 52 = <strong>$9,360/year</strong> spent on work a machine could do</li>
</ul>
<p>Now compare that to a <strong>$4,000</strong> implementation that cuts that to 1 hour a week:</p>
<ul>
<li>You save <strong>$7,800/year</strong> in reclaimed labor — <strong>every single year</strong>.</li>
<li>Payback period: <strong>about 6 months.</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>And that&#39;s before you count the revenue side — the bookings that don&#39;t get missed, the leads that don&#39;t go cold, the no-shows that get filled. For most service businesses, the revenue upside is <em>larger</em> than the labor savings. The labor savings alone usually justify the cost.</p>
<p><strong>Your turn:</strong> grab a pen. Write down the one task your team does most and hates most. Estimate the hours per week and your hourly cost. If the answer is more than 3 hours a week, you almost certainly have an automation worth paying for.</p>
<h2>What Drives the Price Up (and Down)</h2>
<p>To make these numbers feel less arbitrary, here&#39;s what actually moves the needle:</p>
<p><strong>Drives cost UP:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Custom integrations</strong> between tools that don&#39;t natively talk to each other</li>
<li><strong>Complex decision logic</strong> — systems that need to branch and adapt, not just fire-and-forget</li>
<li><strong>Voice and phone agents</strong> (more involved than text/email automation)</li>
<li><strong>High-security industries</strong> (medical, legal, finance) with compliance requirements</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Drives cost DOWN:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clear, documented workflows</strong> — you know exactly what should happen, so the build is faster</li>
<li><strong>Standard tools</strong> (CRMs, calendars, email, spreadsheets) that have ready-made connectors</li>
<li><strong>A single, well-scoped project</strong> instead of &quot;automate everything&quot;</li>
</ul>
<p>The single best way to lower your cost? Know what you want before you call. An owner who can say &quot;I need my no-show rate down and my invoice follow-up automated&quot; will always pay less than one who says &quot;help me with AI.&quot;</p>
<h2>FAQ: AI Automation Cost</h2>
<p><strong>Is AI automation worth it for a small business?</strong>
Almost always — if you pick the right task. The ROI math above shows a $4,000 build paying for itself in six months on labor savings alone, before revenue upside. The key is starting with one high-volume, repetitive workflow, not trying to automate everything at once.</p>
<p><strong>Can I start with just one automation?</strong>
Yes, and you should. A single well-built system — like an AI scheduler or a follow-up sequence — is the smartest first purchase. It proves the ROI and gives you a foundation to build on.</p>
<p><strong>Is it a one-time cost or monthly?</strong>
Both. The audit and implementation are one-time projects. Most businesses then keep a small monthly retainer for maintenance, updates, and monitoring. Budget for the ongoing piece — it&#39;s what keeps your systems from going stale.</p>
<p><strong>Do I have to hire a full agency, or can I learn it myself?</strong>
You can absolutely learn it — that&#39;s what our coaching programs are for. If you have a capable ops person and the time, coaching is the budget-friendly route. If you&#39;d rather own the outcome without building the skills, an agency handles it end-to-end.</p>
<p><strong>Why do some agencies quote $50,000 for the same work?</strong>
Often it&#39;s enterprise positioning, scope creep, or branding, not capability. For a small business, honest scoping usually lands in the $2,500–$10,000 band for implementation. If a quote feels wildly out of line, ask exactly what&#39;s included — and get it in writing.</p>
<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>AI automation isn&#39;t a luxury line item — it&#39;s a decision with a number attached. Run your own math, budget for the one-time build <em>and</em> the monthly upkeep, and start with one workflow you can measure.</p>
